In this lesson we'll look at how to graph a linear relation, which is just an equation that makes a perfectly straight line when you draw it. The easiest form to work with is y equals m x plus c, where m is the slope and c is where the line crosses the y-axis. First, make a table of values. Pick at least three x values, and for each one, work out the matching y. Then plot those points on the Cartesian plane, which is the grid with a horizontal x-axis and a vertical y-axis. Once your points are down, grab a ruler and connect them with a straight line, and remember to extend the line past the outer points so it keeps going in both directions. When you read a graph, the spot where the line crosses the y-axis is your c value, and the slope is the rise over run, basically how much y changes as x increases by one. If you have a spreadsheet or graphing app handy, you can plug in loads of x values fast and get a really accurate picture. One quick tip: spread your x values out, like minus two, zero, and two, so you can see the line's overall direction. Now let's see it in action.